<h2><b>What is EQDamage?</b></h2><BR>
EQ Damage is currently a free service which will process, parse and display in detail the damage you and other characters inflicted upon a mob.  The system allows you to either paste an EQ log into a window or upload the file itself.  There are limitations to the file sizes you can upload however there is a guild to help you get the most from the service with the least amount of work.<BR>
<BR>
<BR>
<h2><b>What are the benifits of EQDmg?</b></h2><BR>
Knowing how much DPS (Damage Per Second) you can produce on a mob is useful for a number of reasons.  Firstly it allows you to compare your characters equipment and class abilities with your peers.  You can measure stat differences and weapon changes against a previously recorded DPS.  Secondly, it is useful for raid leaders and guild leaders to utilise you accordingly.  It also can tell raid leaders on average how high or low the AC (Armour Class) of a mob can be compared with other mobs.  It can tell you how much hit points mobs have.<BR>
<BR>
<BR>
<h2><b>Who can use EQDmg?</b></h2><BR>
Anyone on any server, in any guild or without a guild may use EQDmg.  In fact, anyone who wishes to post a log of a fight may use the system.  People may use the system anonymously or create a login account.  If a person creates a login account then they get an additional benifit of recording and viewing every DPS log they submitted at their leisure.  Anonymous people will be able to parse a log - but not have it at their disposal on the system.<BR>
<BR>
<BR>
<h2><b>Can I share my DPS with other players?</b></h2><BR>
The system allows either anonymous or registered users to publish DPS logs.  Each DPS log published is assigned a forum reference.  At this point the log is public and other people can view the latest submissions and use the search facilities to find vaious entries.   People can post underneath your DPS log comments.  It could be that people question the authenticity of the log and therefore post their views.  Likewise the DPS submitter can reply.
<BR>
<BR>
<h2><b>What if I want to keep my DPS entries private?</b></h2><BR>
If you are an anonymous user after parsing a log and leaving the site you will not be able to see the DPS log again - nor will other people.  If you are an anonymous user and you publish the log it will become private.  If you are a registered user your log is private and stored in your private area.  You may then at any time choose to publish your DPS entrie(s) - in which case they would become public.<BR>
<BR>
